## 2. Decision log — changes since CLAUDE.md

These supersede what's currently written in `CLAUDE.md`. Update that file as part of this build.

| # | Decision | Consequence |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | **Equipment access is removed from the product**, not just the questionnaire. | The plan matrix collapses from 4 goals × 3 equipment tiers (12 templates) to **4 goal templates**. See §2.1 — this one has a real cost. |
| D2 | **No Google Calendar in v1.** | The locked "fixed weekly schedule, missed is missed, never silently rescheduled" decision **stands unchanged**. Calendar remains the long-term goal and the data model should not make it hard to add later. |
| D3 | **Exercise references use `exercise_key`**, not free-text names. | Requires the library repair in §4 before any feature ships. |
| D4 | **Calorie and macro targets are display-only.** | Shown once on the results screen as part of the projection. No logging, no daily tracking, no nutrition screen. Guardrails in §11. |
| D5 | **Questionnaire is one page**, JavaScript swaps which question is visible. | Departs from the multi-page `window.location.href` pattern used so far. |
| D6 | **Stats rise automatically from logged work.** No spendable points. | The `[+]` buttons and "Available Points" from the reference mockup are cut. |
| D7 | **Original naming throughout.** The user's own name from the questionnaire is the character name. | No borrowed character names, rank ladders, or system dialogue. See §10.1. |

### 2.1 The cost of removing equipment access — read this

`CLAUDE.md` called equipment tier the key differentiator and said not to drop it. You've dropped it. That's your call to make, but the consequence should be written down rather than discovered later:

**v1 now assumes the user has access to a fully equipped gym.** The workbook splits prescribe hack squats, pec decks, cable stations, T-bar rows and seated calf machines. A student training in a dorm gym with dumbbells and a bench cannot run these plans. For a campus rec center this is usually a safe assumption; for the dorm-gym user it is not.

Two things soften this without reintroducing the question:

1. **Per-exercise swapping stays and gets more important.** Every slot carries alternatives (§5.3). The framing changes from "you don't own this equipment" to "the rack is taken" — still the single most useful feature at 6pm in a college gym.
2. **The differentiator shifts to schedule-awareness**, which is where Calendar was always headed anyway. Equipment can return later as a profile setting rather than an onboarding question, and the slot/alternative structure means it would slot in without a migration.

